Intent, in one sentence: a hook advisory that flags blanket staging, states the office rule, orders an audit-and-repair of the staged files, and names the exceptions.

Constraints: keep the trigger patterns, the audit commands, every remedy (unstage, amend, revert, including an already-pushed commit), both exceptions, and the trailing III.

<RESULT>
Blanket staging detected (git add -A/--all/-u/. or git commit -a). Office rule: commit with --only, staging only the whole files this conversation touched. In a shared checkout, a blanket add sweeps sibling sessions' work-in-progress and scratch files into your commit. Before building on this command, list what it staged (git status / git show --stat HEAD) and claim each file as this session's own; unstage, amend away, or revert the rest, including a commit this same command already pushed. A sweep the user ordered, or a fresh import where everything is yours: carry on. III.
</RESULT>

Key edits: "noted" became "detected"; "The office rule is:" compressed to "Office rule:"; "nothing else" folded into "staging only"; "whatever is not" became "the rest". All commands, remedies, and both exceptions are unchanged; reduction is under 20%, so no delta report.
